  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "my washin mashine wont turn on at all no lights nothin"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "A washing machine with absolutely no lights or response is usually caused by a loss of mains power rather than an internal fault. The most common reasons are simply that the socket has no power (perhaps due to a tripped fuse or RCD), the plug is loose, or the lead itself is damaged. Please check if the machine is plugged directly into a wall outlet or via an extension lead. If it's in a standard socket, try plugging in a lamp or phone charger to confirm that socket has power. Let me know what happens."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "its built in behind a cupboard door so i cant see the plug. but my kettle and fridge work fine in the kitchen"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Before you start, switch it off and unplug it. Since you can't see the plug behind the cupboard, please pull the machine out just enough to check that it is firmly plugged in and that the cable isn't damaged or cut. If the connection looks fine but there's still no power, we'll need the brand and model number (usually on a sticker inside the door rim or on the back of the door) to identify the specific internal components like the mains filter or PCB.\n[Panel: \"To check a suitable replacement — Enter your model number below. Once we have the model I can check whether a suitable replacement is available. It's usually on a sticker inside the door or around the frame, or on the back of the appliance.\"]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "i cant pull it out, its built in and im not allowed to take the cupboard off. i dont have any tools either. i dont know the brand or model, the name on the door edge is worn off and i cant find a label"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "A completely dead machine with power at the wall usually indicates an internal fault, such as a failed mains filter on the circuit board. Because this requires opening the appliance casing to test internal components, you will need to call a qualified engineer to diagnose and repair it."
  }
 ],
